I graduated college feeling like a total  boss-lady Club leadership student  government and let's not forget one of  those fancy management consulting jobs  you know them I walked across that stage  at graduation feeling ready to take on  the world within a year though that  confidence had disappeared I watched as  each one of my peers got staffed on cool  new projects while I sat and staffed  after each project interview that  resulted in no project offer I asked  myself what's wrong with me I don't they  want you when I eventually did get  staffed my manager was cold and critical  I remember my heart beating as I walked  to his desk to ask a question making  myself small and unobtrusive  this timidity followed me to my second  project but this one had a lot more  client interaction and all of a sudden  my feedback read lacks executive  presence I internalized this feedback  but without a mentor to show me how it  was done or somebody giving me guidance  on how to actually change all I did was  keep fixating on the fact that I was  inadequate I didn't feel ready to take  on my job much less the world anymore  my confidence was gone that intrinsic  untouchable belief in myself that I had  started my career with had vanished  replaced by fear that I wasn't enough  doubt that I had a future in the  business world and jealousy of everyone  around me who seemed to fit in and have  it figure it out my confidence stayed  low even as my performance improved  enough to get me here to the GSP with  all of you incredible people during my  MBA one year I stumbled across some  research that changed my own perspective  on my experiences it turns out what I  had gone through starting my career with  relatively high confidence and then  losing it with time that's actually  startling ly normal women enter the  workforce with confidence roughly equal  to that of their male peers  whit's high men's confidence remains  steady but the number of women who  believe they have what it takes to  succeed is cut in half it's something  called the confidence cliff this  phenomenon in which women actually lose  confidence once they've started work  there's a lot of potential explanations  everything from a lack of female role  models and mentors in the workplace  lessons taught to young girls about what  it means to be a good girl the  transition from an educational  experience that's relatively  straightforward to a work environment  that's politically fraught and which  like favors like all of these factors  and more come together to trip women up  until they have fallen off the cliff  and look this isn't something happening  out there to faceless nameless people it  happens here too in fact when I surveyed  the women in our community almost 90  percent said that they had experienced a  moderate or big decline in confidence 90  percent okay this sucks  we know that confidence is critical for  success without it women are less  ambitious less impactful and less likely  to get that next big promotion but  here's the thing when women lose we all  lose our organizations miss out on the  talent of half of their talent pool and  our leadership becomes less diverse with  worse outcomes as a result but beyond  the organizational impact and the  professional impact it also just fuels  really shitty I hate the feeling of  doubting myself  I hate that in addition to doing my job  I'm also fighting this battle with  confidence but more than anything I hate  the thought of each new generation of  women falling off the same cliff that  we've been falling off of for  generations this has to stop for the  class of 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 and  beyond for them I'm standing up here  today and I invite you to stand with me  look right here in this room we have the  future business leaders of the world and  an awful lot of people who are going to  be managing teams in the next few years  when surveyed every single one of the  women said that having a supportive  manager or mentor would have had a  significant impact on her confidence of  those half so that it would even be  game-changing you have the ability to be  game-changing for the young woman on  your team don't be like my first manager  who didn't even see that I was falling  off the confidence cliff right in front  of him get to know that young woman on  her team  take her out to coffee mentor her or  introduce her to someone who can realize  that your sponsorship will be  game-changing not just for others see  her but for how she sees herself and  look all of your energy effort and time  on this will pay back to you tenfold not  only will you get a more effective  member of your team  but her loyalty to you will propel you  through the organization I know that all  of you are wonderful and you're gonna go  out there and be incredible leaders who  fight the confidence cliff along with  the women on your teams but we've also  got to start talking to the women  themselves look this is the group of  people who are so powerful so confident  when they start their careers we've got  to give them the tools and insight they  need to keep that confidence  so for all the women in this room who  might be trying to overcome the  confidence cliff and even for the 21  year-olds on the other side of that  camera still feeling on top of the world  and about to start your first big job in  a few months you are powerful you can  take on the world if I could give any  advice to my 21 year old self as I  walked across that stage at graduation  this is what I would say first find your  mentor and get advice on how to guide  your particular manager to be a better  Ally for you second don't fall into that  same critical thinking that I had during  the first few years of my career instead  see feedback as an opportunity for  growth I know I know  feedbacks a gift right but really though  now I realized that it was my fixed  mindset that meant that I internalized  the feedback I was given as a  condemnation of my potential and thus a  blow to my confidence instead practice  growth mindset which is all about  embracing your ability and even your  imperative to evolve and if that growth  takes a while go easy on yourself and  last get your awareness up for me just  knowing the confidence cliff was a thing  made me a less guilty for experiencing  it but be more motivated to rebuild my  confidence I didn't want to be just  another stat about gender inequality for  you  I hope knowing about the cliff makes you  more proactive and fighting it but also  lets you know you're not alone  we're all fighting this together on  behalf of all the generations of women  that we will bring up behind us so  overcome the confidence cliff and go  forward into the world I believe in you  I'll keep believing in yourself thank  you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

